{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Delete the two bridge verbs that are dead on arrival: `rebar bridge-status` and `rebar purge-bridge`.\nThis is pure dead-code deletion. It is carved out of `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a` (\"Establish the `bridge`\nverb group\") so it can land immediately, with NO dependency on any sibling — nothing in the new\n`bridge` surface replaces either verb, so nothing has to ship first.\n## Why each verb is dead\n**`bridge-status`** — its producer has never existed in this repo. The reader consumes\n`<tracker>/.bridge-status.json`, but rebar was extracted from digital-service-orchestra on\n2026-06-08 and the reader was a knowing tombstone from day one; on every post-cutover repo it exits\n1 (\"No bridge status file found\"). Verified: zero references to the producer in `docs/`, `scripts/`\nor `.github/`. Note also that the citation in its module docstring — \"epic 3a03\" — is DANGLING: in\nthis repo `3a03` resolves to an unrelated test-isolation bug created 11 days AFTER the comment\nciting it. The real epic is `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`. That dangling citation dies with the module, so\nthe \"correct the 3a03 citation\" obligation from `4752` is discharged here for this file.\n**`purge-bridge`** — five commits, every one an automated lint sweep or safety retrofit, none a\nfeature or a bug fix, and no script, workflow, or runbook invokes it. Its `--keep` flag is also\ninverted-sense (it deletes everything NOT matching), which is exactly the kind of surface the\nbridge redesign is removing rather than renaming.\n## User-facing strings retire WITH the verbs (carried from `4752` section B6)\nRetiring `bridge-status` leaves two strings pointing at a verb that no longer exists:\n* `src/rebar/_engine_support/bridge.py:32` — `_USAGE = \"Usage: ticket bridge-status [--output json]\"`\n  (that module IS the `bridge-status` implementation, so it retires whole, `_USAGE` included);\n* `src/rebar/schemas/bridge_status.schema.json:5`, whose description names\n  `rebar bridge-status --output json`. The schema file is retired outright, since sibling\n  `b69c-5787-81b8-4052` builds `bridge status` on durable state and publishes its own schema.\n`purge-bridge` likewise carries `src/rebar/_commands/purge_bridge.py:37`\n(`_USAGE = \"Usage: ticket-purge-bridge.sh --keep=<PROJECT_KEY> [--dry-run]\"` — itself a stale\nreference to a bash script deleted at the Tier-E cutover) and `src/rebar/_cli/help/purge-bridge.txt`.\n## Enumerated consumers (all deletions or edits, no migrations)\nCLI wiring: `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py` — the `_BRIDGE` frozenset at line 121 (drop\n`bridge-status` and `purge-bridge`, keep `bridge-fsck`), the `bridge-status` arm at ~305 and the\n`purge-bridge` arm at ~312.\nHelp: `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt` (lines naming both verbs),\n`src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge-status.txt`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/purge-bridge.txt`.\nImplementation: `src/rebar/_engine_support/bridge.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/purge_bridge.py`.\nSchemas: `src/rebar/schemas/bridge_status.schema.json`.\nTests (delete, they only cover the retired verbs): `tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_status.py`,\n`tests/unit/test_purge_bridge_locked_commit.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_purge_bridge_prune_concurrency.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/store/_purge_race_worker.py`.\nContract tests (edit): `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_schema_coverage.py` — the\n... (22 lines truncated)\n[see remaining: tail -n +41 \"$HOME/Library/Application Support/rtk/tee/1786118226_jq_-r__description.log\"]\n\n## Complete consumer inventory and verification oracle\n\nThe deletion also updates consumers omitted from the initial inventory:\n\n- `src/rebar/schemas/__init__.py`: remove the `BRIDGE_STATUS` export, constant, and\n  `OUTPUT_SCHEMAS[\"bridge_status\"]` entry together with `bridge_status.schema.json`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine_support/reads_cli.py`: replace both live\n  `Run: ticket bridge-status for details.` warnings with neutral guidance to inspect unresolved\n  bridge alerts. Do not point at `bridge status`, because that sibling command has not landed yet.\n- `src/rebar/_engine_support/output.py`: remove `bridge-status` from the report-profile\n  documentation.\n- `docs/output-schemas.md`: remove the `bridge-status` to `bridge_status` row.\n- `CHANGELOG.md`: add release notes for both retirements; existing historical entries remain\n  intentionally.\n\nThe B6 oracle is exact: a behavioral contract test proves both retired CLI tokens are unknown and\nabsent from top-level help, then asserts zero `bridge-status` or `purge-bridge` matches in the\nshipped package tree `src/rebar` and the active command-contract docs `docs/cli-reference.md`,\n`docs/exit-codes.md`, and `docs/output-schemas.md`. Tests, `CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/archive` are\nexcluded because they intentionally retain specification or historical evidence.\n\nAdditional independently verifiable acceptance criteria:\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge-status` exits as an unknown command.\n- [ ] `rebar purge-bridge` exits as an unknown command.\n- [ ] Neither retired command appears in `rebar --help`.\n- [ ] The schema registry has no `BRIDGE_STATUS` export, constant, or output-schema entry.\n- [ ] Both surviving read warnings provide neutral bridge-alert guidance and name no retired or\n      not-yet-shipped command.\n- [ ] The shipped-package and active-doc zero-match oracle passes.\n- [ ] `CHANGELOG.md` records both retirements."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786118226589200001,"uuid":"3282a212-bf25-4ec5-b87f-e04e0ed51c8a"}